                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Agents of Mayhem squanders its stylish Saturday-morning cartoon premise on one of the most tedious gameplay loops in modern action games. While switching between eccentric heroes in combat offers brief flashes of fun, the experience quickly collapses under relentless repetition. The mission structure is painfully formulaic, forcing you to hack the same consoles, fight identical waves of grunt enemies, and clear the same objectives ad nauseam. Worse yet, the indoor dungeon levels—specifically the ubiquitous underground LEGION lairs—are blatantly copy-pasted, making every subterranean breach look and feel identical to the last. This structural monotony completely drains any sense of progression or discovery from the campaign. Up on the surface, the futuristic Seoul setting functions as an entirely unnecessary pseudo-open world that does nothing to justify its existence. The city is essentially a lifeless, sterile backdrop devoid of meaningful emergent activities, memorable pedestrian AI, or rewarding side content. Traversing these empty streets quickly feels like an unwanted chore designed merely to pad out playtime between disjointed mission markers. Rather than capturing the anarchic sandbox energy of the developer’s Saints Row lineage, the world feels hollow and detached. Ultimately, a game that desperately aims for chaotic, blockbuster spectacle ends up drowning in sheer, mind-numbing predictability.\n\n[b]RATING: 4/10[/b]",
